5. Dead Battery Boot Issue & Solution
Problem: Very dead batteries caused the system to draw ~3mA and never boot. Root cause traced to nRF chip drawing excessive current on high-impedance power supplies, holding input voltage at the regulator enable threshold (1.58V).

Solution implemented:

Added bulk capacitance across battery terminals (dead batteries have very high impedance due to protection circuit)

Added 470kΩ/100kΩ resistor divider + 1µF capacitor on regulator enable pin

Note: Alternative regulators were investigated but current unit was retained due to cost considerations.

6. Coil Detection Feature
The charger implements coil detection to identify when an orthotic is present:

Detection mechanism: Mutual resonance causes sense voltage increase when coils are co-located

LED indication: Yellow (appears green) → White when orthotic detected

Alternative colour scheme available: Red → Blue (if preferred)

Testing Results (4 units):

2 units: Functioning as expected

1 unit: Detection threshold too low

1 unit: Detection threshold too high

Recommendation: Migrate coil detection from PWM-off sensing to single excitation pulse method. Coil detection currently disabled to prevent charging issues.

7. Build & Assembly Notes
7.1 Programming
All 4 units successfully flashed via UPDI interface (J2 connector)

One unit had glue in programming holes - required additional effort

7.2 Assembly Procedure
Plug housing LED hole with clear hot glue

Insert PCBA into housing, apply hot glue through mounting holes

Mount coil with 0.6mm thick VHB tape

Route and secure coil wires

Install magnet with S pole facing outward (critical for proper alignment)

Fit test against orthotic device

Pot coil area with black hot glue, keeping layer thin over magnet

Smooth with hot air, cool with fan

Fill any voids (especially around magnet) with additional hot glue after cooling/settling

